WEBVTT

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.450
I was talking to a guy at a party, an older man, and this guy's opinionated and very charming.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:10.340
And we're talking about politics, and the guy gets on that he's a communist, an actual member of the actual communist party.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:12.580
So how's it going?

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:13.660
I ask him.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:14.910
The cause of world communism.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:23.620
And he talks about the media not giving communism a fair shake, and he pulls out a pamphlet that he gives me for some talk that a fellow traveler is giving.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.400
I asked him about the collapse of the Soviet Union in the Eastern Bloc.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.990
Didn't that put a kind of damper on the whole worldwide movement?

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:41.800
And he said what communists have been saying for decades, really, that the Soviet Union's not real communism, that no one's tried real communism the way that it's supposed to be tried.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:50.120
And suddenly I felt like I was talking to a time traveler, to somebody for whom the last few decades had not occurred or simply didn't matter.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:50.920
her.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.420
Marcel Elfeld spent years making movies about Nazis, The Sorrow and the Pity, Memory of Justice.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.420
His family was Jewish, escaped Germany before the war.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.140
What's it like spending so much time interviewing Nazis, he was asked once.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.020
Oh, I get along with Nazis, he said.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.840
We share something in common, an interest in the past.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.600
I share more with them than I do with most people today who don't care about the past.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.600
Nearly a decade ago, I had a chance to interview the members of a band called Camper Van Beethoven.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:27.040
At that time, they were one of my favorite bands, but I sort of, I blew my interview with them because I misunderstood them and their music so grossly.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.200
I was out of touch.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:31.760
I will just say that right now.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:38.800
They were playing a kind of country folk mixed with surfer music, mixed with the sort of fake Egyptian, Russian music, whatever else kind of came to mind, ska.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.360
They openly sang about drug use.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:53.730
And this was before our culture, before we as a people had coined the words slacker or Gen X. And I spent a lot of that interview alienating the guys in this band by asking, were they hippies?

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.000
Were they modern-day hippies?

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:58.940
That was the only phrase I had in my head to refer to what I was seeing.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:00.980
I thought they were stuck in the wrong decade.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.100
But in fact, they were just carrying a few props from another decade into the present.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.800
Which is, in fact, what I think we all do.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:18.900
Walk down Michigan Avenue or State Street or Wabash here in Chicago, and you'll see older guys in wide -brimmed hats from the 50s coming out of the off-track bedding.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:24.860
There are the Art Institute students dressed in that fake 70s thing we've been suffering through as a nation for the last three years.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:32.660
We all carry pieces of the past, some of us more than others, which brings us to today's radio program.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.360
From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it is This American Life.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:38.100
I'm Ira Glass.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.860
Today on our program, people stuck in another decade.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.200
Act one, the wedding game.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.980
Two people who love 1970s television and how it affects their engagement and their wedding.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.020
Act two, stuck as a teenager.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.320
Two people who get suspended in time as adolescents for very different reasons.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.140
Act three, perv walk.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.160
Two smart, funny 90s men who have no problem working with women or seeing women as their equals.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:06.660
And why?

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:13.700
They spend 10 10 minutes a day walking around scoping women and talking about them like they were Jack Lemmon in some 1950s movie.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:26.960
Act four, working in another decade, in which our intrepid correspondent takes a job at Mount Vernon, George Washington's plantation home, doing a job they politely call field hand.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:35.620
Act five, nostalgia, the final frontier, someone who is stuck in a decade that is as far back as you can possibly go.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:42.180
No. Back in the day before back in the day, the day when all matter in the universe existed in a single point in space.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:44.820
You remember that.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:46.640
Stay with us.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:54.900
Equine.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:56.240
The winning game.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:04.520
One common way, perhaps the most common way these days, that people get stuck in other decades is that they get stuck in the TV shows of other decades.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:07.300
Consider, please, this extreme case example.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.120
Carl began his life with the normal, excessive relationship that most of us have with television.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.940
As a child, he watched six or seven hours a day.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.340
Wendy went even further.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.620
She actually watched TV to see herself.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:25.200
As a kid growing up in Chicago, she appeared in commercials that were shot here, for McDonald's, for Sears, for Kellogg's.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.380
She was even in a Morris the Cat commercial.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.880
Wendy knows Morris the Cat.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:35.540
I worked with Morris, and they actually showed my face, which was the first time for something like that.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:36.320
Do you have a speaking part?

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:37.800
Yeah, I had a line.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.200
It was, oh, already?

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.340
And Carl says he knows the commercial line.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.080
I remember that.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:42.880
I love that spot.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:44.000
I think that's so cute.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:45.060
Do it the right way, though.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:45.320
Do it.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:47.540
It was, you know, the mother would say, Morris, time for din-din.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.180
And I went, oh, already?

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:51.340
Wow.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:58.540
Years of watching the Dick Van Dyke Show and That Girl and old game shows gave Wendy these kinds of skills.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:07.560
Carl says that once he turned on an episode of the Mary Tyler Moore Show for five seconds, turned off the TV, and asked Wendy to identify the episode and tell him the plot.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.860
She described everything that would happen, scene by scene.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.340
And this turns out to be a marketable job skill.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:25.820
It was for a station in Chicago that was launching, and they were going to run old shows, and I got the job by writing scripts for promos based on episodes and lines I remembered.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.160
So I didn't even have to see the shows, and I could write scripts for them.

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:37.100
So I grew up inside the television, and I just kind of wanted to stay there, like Mike TV on Willy Wonka.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:37.680
That's me.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:39.740
And I always wanted to be there.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.680
I would watch Zoom and all the shows with kids and just want to be there so bad I could taste it.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.720
That's why I watched a lot of TV. I just wanted to do it.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:54.200
So many people like 70-Zero TV, but the two of you have taken it further throughout the entire courtship process.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.920
Carl, can I just ask you to explain very briefly how you chose to propose to Wendy?

00:05:59.300 --> 00:05:59.700
Sure.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:12.140
Well, you know, one of the reasons that Wendy said that she liked me was that I could recite the lines from a song that was on the Jetsons called Eep Op Ork Ah Ah, and that means I love you.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:20.120
So when it came time to actually get engaged, I thought, well, maybe I could use something that relates to television to do it.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:31.540
and I was watching an episode of The Flintstones and it was the episode where Barney buys a ring for Betty and he gives it to Fred and says, could you please hide this for me?

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:35.780
So Fred hides it in his bowling ball and Wilma finds it and thinks that it's her ring.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:41.060
So Wendy and I love to bowl and I thought, well, I'll hide it in her bowling ball and we'll go bowling.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.720
I knew exactly what he was doing when I found the ring in the bag.

00:06:43.780 --> 00:07:31.240
I knew what he was referring to because the whole day he had bought this Flintstones CD, like music from the show and he was playing it throughout the day and acting very strangely and I obviously didn't know what was going on but it all came together when I pulled the ring out of the bowling bag and you saw the ring in the bowling bag and you just know oh he's doing the Flintstones episode yeah absolutely we're so weird But all of this was just prelude, just the hors d'oeuvre, just the 30-second promo that leads up to the very special episode of whatever that all came before Carl and Wendy's wedding.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.460
The invitation was a great big TV set, and in the middle it said, This is not a test, folks.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.780
Wendy and Carl T. are actually getting married.

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:42.280
Dinner, dancing, and must-see matrimony is what we called it.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:08:14.080
ladies and gentlemen it's time to play the wedding game let's have a big hand for our host whatever this may sound like it's carl and wendy's wedding video they staged the ceremony as a tv game show we're going to ask 10 questions and they're going to have to reach 100 points in order to get married here tonight.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.180
And, Karen, where are you, Karen?

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:22.160
Here I am, Judge.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:24.160
Who is our first contestant?

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:27.360
Well, Judge, we have from our Vista, California...

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.480
Well, it was very simple.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:09:10.420
The night before, and everybody can't believe that we did this the night before, we asked each other a series of questions that we had to answer on cards much like they do on the newlywed game and we didn't tell each other what we what our answers were going to be and then we gave the judge who was going to marry us who's a great guy um we gave him the questions and he was to ask us these questions and we were going to answer truthfully and see if we could get enough points to get married anyway uh and which one are you wendy i am well this is on wendy wendy and tell me something about yourself i'm a house We both knew that it was going to be funny.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:17.620
So we didn't even need to rig it or set it up because I just knew that whatever he put down or what I put down was going to make it entertaining.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.680
And I thought the element of suspense was also really good.

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:21.700
So we didn't cheat at all.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:24.460
I didn't know what he was saying and he didn't know what I was saying.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:25.970
And so it was totally played legitimately.

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:32.040
Now we did plan to do poorly, but I think we did more poorly than I expected.

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:36.770
Yeah, there were a couple things I thought for sure I'm sure we would have gotten it right if we didn't.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:38.520
We're whipping along here.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.160
We're going to question number six.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.440
Carl, complete this sentence.

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:50.220
My fiancée is great, but her blank leaves something to be desired.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:52.500
Carl, answer.

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:53.980
What do you think you would say?

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.120
Well, you know, there's so many things that are great about Wendy.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:58.080
Way to go.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:09:59.960
And I love her dearly.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:00.600
Way to go.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.840
But her temper leaves something to be desired sometimes.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:08.380
Every once in a while.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:09.160
No way.

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:10.100
What do you say?

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:11.120
What?

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:12.440
He says temper.

