I said Best Friends.

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:39.160
Best Friends.

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:41.920
Burt Reynolds, Goldie Hawn.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:43.260
Burt Reynolds, Goldie Hawn.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:44.660
And Wendy?

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:46.520
Gone with the Wind.

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:48.920
Well, let's go on with the ten points, too.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:52.940
We've been together eight years.

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:53.720
I mean, you know.

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:54.240
Right.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:58.240
My main concern was that it was funny.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:12:59.400
That's all I cared about.

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:01.380
As long as we got laughs, I was happy.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:06.020
So I answered questions that I knew would hopefully get laughs, and luckily they did.

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:07.620
But I didn't care about it.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:08.200
I wasn't nervous.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:10.560
I didn't care about marrying, you know, the wrong guy.

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:13.010
I knew I was with the right guy, and I knew I was in the right place.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:17.740
So all I really cared about was the entertainment value of the game portion of the evening, really.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:18.820
That's all I thought about.

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:32.540
And the producer in me was worried that it was going too slowly, that he wasn't getting the right camera angles, and, you know, oh, yeah, the questions, yeah, I'm not worried about that, but it was the show part and that people were having a good time.

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:34.840
Yeah, I wish we had more cameras, actually.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:36.800
That would be good.

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:41.860
But you buy the hair stuff and the cleaning stuff and the moisturizer.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:43.780
Okay, so we're going to have to move this along.

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:44.500
You've got to get married.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:45.080
Come on.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:46.440
Well, maybe not.

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:47.720
Maybe not.

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:48.380
That's true.

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:49.160
This is true.

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:53.580
When I heard what you were doing, I mean, there was a part of me where I was just like, wow, they are amazing.

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:56.360
And there was a part of me which is just like, are they nuts?

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.600
Well, what would be amazing about it?

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:00.560
What would be nuts about it?

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:11.540
Well, because a wedding at some point, I mean, I think it has to be about love, about this, you know, just about this feeling between the two of you, you know?

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:16.120
And the context of, you know, your typical game show doesn't usually make the space for that.

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:22.550
I mean, I've been to some weddings, some of our friends, and I just thought they were beautiful and romantic.

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:24.680
And I thought, wow, I would love to do that.

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.600
But I also thought that that really wasn't us.

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:29.780
I mean, it's not how we deal with each other.

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:35.500
Not that we're not romantic and love each other, but we just express it in a different way.

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:38.600
You know, I've been to some weddings that really sucked.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.160
I mean, you know, I mean, come on.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:46.540
There have been some totally lame-ass weddings at a banquet hall in, you know, Lamont, Illinois, or Joliet.

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:48.600
They go on for an hour and a half.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.150
Yeah, and people think they're romantic, and they're just boring.

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:57.360
So, you know, anyone could, you know, anyone could do what it takes to, quote, unquote, have a romantic wedding.

00:14:57.460 --> 00:14:58.600
It's just like making soup.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:14:59.540
You know, you have the right ingredients.

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:00.970
You throw in the flowers.

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:01.940
You throw in the dress.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.600
You throw in someone playing a song from Fiddler on the Roof or whatever.

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:06.480
You just, you know, you throw in all that.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:07.840
I'm not doing any wedding for my family.

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:11.800
Well, yeah, it's like you throw in all the usual crap, and that's supposed to be romantic.

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:13.820
Well, that's, you know, that's not true.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:20.000
And nobody, I don't think anybody really said, people actually said to me, this is exactly the wedding I thought you would have.

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:22.540
And that, to me, says everything right there.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:24.740
Was it romantic at all?

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:41.360
I think the most romantic part, and maybe it wasn't for the audience, but for me, and maybe for us, because it was very romantic during the actual ceremony itself when the judge broke it all down to what it was really all about.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:45.600
When he said that the fact that we had found each other was a miracle.

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:54.400
I mean, it is unusual that two very different people found each other through the biggest medium in the world, which is television.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:16:01.620
And it brought us together and it brought a whole bunch of people that we love together that wouldn't normally know each other.

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:01.720
there.

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:04.760
I don't think you could say that there's a traditional romance.

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:11.120
I mean, this is, to make it romantic is kind of to do what you want to do and to do what means the most to you.

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:12.450
And I think we achieved that.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:15.660
